course: money
lesson: 5
title: Money's history: shells, coins, paper, cards, phones
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Money has changed its shape many times, but its job has stayed the same: to buy, to save and to measure what things are worth.
explain: Long ago people used things that were hard to find and that everyone agreed on, like cowrie shells, salt, cattle and beads; today a bucket of beach shells buys nothing, because nobody agrees to use shells as money any more.
explain: About 2,600 years ago the first coins were made in Lydia, in what is now Turkey, and over 1,000 years ago China began using paper money, because a light note was easier to carry than a heavy pile of coins.
explain: Then came cheques, bank cards and phones: when Mum taps her phone to pay £3 for a coffee, no coins move, but the number in her bank account goes from £60 to £57, and the money is just as real.
fact: Long ago, in parts of Africa and Asia, small shiny cowrie shells were used as money.
fact: Salt was very valuable because it stopped food going bad; the word "salary" comes from the Latin word for salt, though historians argue about whether Roman soldiers were really paid in salt.
fact: The first coins were made about 2,600 years ago in Lydia, a land in what is now Turkey.
fact: Coins made trade easier because each one had a set size, weight and value, so people did not have to weigh metal every time.
fact: China was the first country to use paper money, over 1,000 years ago.
fact: A cheque is a written note telling your bank to pay someone; few people use cheques now.
fact: Paying by card or phone uses the money in a bank account; no coins move, but the numbers in the bank change.
fact: On the island of Yap, giant stone wheels were used as money; some were too big to move, so people simply remembered who owned each one.
fact: In the UK, the £1 coin replaced the £1 note in the 1980s, because coins last much longer than notes.
fact: Cash use is falling fast, but many people, including some older people and people without bank cards, still need it; people disagree about how fast cash should go.
fact: Some shops already test paying with your face or fingerprint.
fact: "Minted" is British slang for very rich.
exercise: What were cowrie shells used for long ago in parts of Africa and Asia? | As money | As plates | As jewellery only | As building bricks
exercise: Kai has a bucket of 50 beach shells. How many sweets do they buy today? | None, because nobody uses shells as money now | 50 sweets, one sweet for each shell he brings in | 25 sweets, because the shop takes two shells each | As many as he wants
exercise: Which English word comes from the Latin word for salt? | Salary | Salad | Silver | Sale
exercise: Why was salt so valuable long ago? | It kept food from going bad | It was used to make coins | It was the only thing people could eat | It was shiny like gold
exercise: Where were the first coins made, about 2,600 years ago? | In Lydia, in what is now Turkey | In London, England, under the first kings | In Rome, Italy, in Julius Caesar's time | In Kyiv, Ukraine
exercise: Why did coins make trade easier? | Each coin had a set value, so nobody had to weigh metal | Coins could be eaten in an emergency | Coins were lighter than paper | Coins were free for everyone
exercise: Which country first used paper money, over 1,000 years ago? | China | Britain | America | France
exercise: Why was paper money useful? | One light note could stand for many heavy coins | Paper is stronger than metal, so notes never got lost | Paper never wears out, even after years in pockets | Paper was worth more than gold
exercise: A soldier gets one bag of salt a month. How many bags in a year? | 12 | 10 | 52 | 365
exercise: What is a cheque? | A written note telling your bank to pay someone | A coin used in Britain before the pound was invented | A plastic card you tap on a machine to pay without cash | A receipt from a shop showing what you have just bought
exercise: Grandad writes a cheque for £25 to the plumber. What happens? | The bank moves £25 from Grandad's account to the plumber's | The plumber gets £25 of paper to keep | The bank prints a new £25 note | Nothing, because cheques are not real money
exercise: Mum has £60 in the bank and taps her phone to pay £3. How much is left? | £57 | £63 | £53 | £60
exercise: On Yap, how did people "spend" a stone too big to move? | Everyone agreed the stone now had a new owner | They rolled it all the way to the new owner's house | They broke it into small pieces to use as coins | They threw it into the sea to keep it safe
exercise: Count the timeline: shells, coins, paper, cheques, cards, phones. How many steps? | 6 | 5 | 4 | 7
exercise: Why do some people say cash should not disappear? | Some people do not have bank cards or phones | Cash is the only real money, and cards are just pretend | Cards stop working every winter when it gets too cold | Shops make much more money when people pay with cash
exercise: Why did Britain swap the £1 note for a £1 coin? | Coins last much longer than notes used every day | Notes were too heavy to carry around in a purse or pocket | Coins are cheaper to fake | People stopped liking paper
test: Kai goes "I'm minted, got a bucket of shells!" ("Minted" means rich.) What's the truth? | Shells were money long ago, but nobody takes them now | He's right, shells are still money everywhere in the world | He's right if the shells are big enough | Shells were never used as money anywhere
test: Isla asks "where'd the money go? I didn't see any!" after Mum taps her phone. What's the answer? | It left Mum's bank account, as numbers changing | It's hidden inside the phone until the battery runs out | It wasn't real money, so nothing was paid | The coffee shop gave it back
test: Zac's like "no cash in the future, bro, it's dead." His aunt says her old neighbour still needs it. Who's right? | Both have a point: cash is falling, but some people still need it | Only Zac, cash is already gone everywhere | Only the aunt, cash is growing fast | Neither, cash was banned last year
test: A cartoon shows cavemen paying with credit cards. What's wrong? | Cards came thousands of years later; early people used things like shells | Nothing, cavemen had the first bank cards | Cavemen used phones, not cards | Cards were invented before coins
test: Teacher: "The first coins were made 2,600 years ago." Liam: "Older than my nan then." If a lifetime is about 100 years, how many lifetimes back is that? | About 26 | About 2 | About 260 | About 6
test: Rosa's mate says "Roman soldiers 100% got paid in salt, that's a fact." What's more careful to say? | The word salary comes from salt, but historians argue about the payment story | Yes, every Roman soldier got only salt | No, Romans never used salt at all | Romans were paid in phones
test: Ethan says the next step is "paying with your face, innit." Is that made up? | No, some shops already test face and fingerprint payment | Yes, it's only in films | Yes, because a face can never be linked to a bank account at all | No, everyone already pays with their face
test: Theo's nan, a West Country lass, says "we 'ad paper pound notes, my lover, gurt big ones." ("My lover" is a friendly greeting; "gurt" means big.) What replaced them? | The £1 coin, in the 1980s | The £5 note, last year | The 50p coin, in Roman times | Gold bars, in the 1900s
test: What would happen if every coin had a different weight and no number on it? | People would have to weigh and argue over each coin | Shopping would be much faster, because nobody would need to count | Prices would become free, since coins would have no fixed value | Nothing would change, because coins are never checked by anyone
test: Grandad's chequebook: Josh says "what the heck is that, a menu?" What's it for? | Writing notes that tell the bank to pay people | Writing down a shopping list before going to the market | Keeping old receipts tidy in a box in case of refunds | Drawing pictures of money to teach children to count
test: Pippa: "how'd they spend a stone bigger than a car?" Which modern money is a bit like that? | Bank money, where only the record of who owns it changes | Coins, which you hand over in a shop | Shells, which you carry in a bucket | Sweets, which you swap at break
exam: Identify the location wherein, according to established historical evidence, the earliest coins were produced. | Lydia, a territory now forming part of Turkey | The Roman province of Britannia, under the emperors | The island of Yap in the Pacific Ocean | The kingdom of England under the Tudors
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a cheque? | A written instruction to a bank to effect payment to a named party | A metallic token of fixed value issued by a mint | A card permitting payment by contact with a terminal | A receipt issued by a merchant following a sale
exam: It is given that an account holds sixty pounds and that a payment of three pounds is subsequently effected by telephone. Ascertain the balance remaining. | Fifty-seven pounds | Sixty-three pounds | Fifty-three pounds | Sixty pounds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of paper money. | It was first employed in China more than one thousand years ago | It was first employed in Britain in the present century | It was first employed by the Romans in place of salt | It was first employed on the island of Yap
